<LegalPage
  appName="Arbora"
  docTitle="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Arbora handles your data: what the app collects, why, who processes it, and your rights under the GDPR."
  canonicalUrl="https://rubenspessoa.dev/arbora/privacy/"
  effectiveDate="July 22, 2026"
>
  <p>
    This policy explains what data the Arbora iOS app collects, why, who processes it, and the
    rights you have. We collect as little as we can get away with: the app works without an
    account, your plant photos are not stored on our servers, and we do not sell or share your
    data for advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    Rubens Pessoa de Barros Filho
    <br />
    UNESCO-Esplanade 8/55, 8020 Graz, Austria
    <br />
    Email: <a href="mailto:contact@rubenspessoa.dev">contact@rubenspessoa.dev</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    See the <a href="/imprint/">imprint</a> for full legal details.
  </p>

  <h2>What we collect and why</h2>

  <h3>Plant photos</h3>
  <p>
    When you identify or diagnose a plant, your photo is sent to our servers and processed through
    the API of a specialized third-party AI service, solely to generate the identification or
    diagnosis. The photo carries no account identifier, is not used to train AI models under our
    API agreements, and is not stored on our servers: it is processed in memory and discarded once
    your result is generated. Photos you save in the app are stored on your device and, if you use
    iCloud, synced to your own private iCloud storage, which we cannot access.
  </p>

  <h3>Anonymous device identity</h3>
  <p>
    The app creates an anonymous identifier via Firebase (Google) when you first launch it. No
    email, name, or password is involved. We use this identifier to enforce fair usage limits,
    connect your subscription to your installation, and count usage in analytics. We cannot
    identify you personally from it.
  </p>

  <h3>Purchases</h3>
  <p>
    Subscriptions are sold and billed by Apple through the App Store. We never see your payment
    details. RevenueCat (USA) processes your App Store transaction status together with the
    anonymous identifier so the app knows whether the paid tier is active.
  </p>

  <h3>Usage analytics</h3>
  <p>
    We use PostHog, hosted in the European Union, to understand how the app is used: screens
    viewed, features used, and events such as a completed scan (including the identified species
    name, which is data about your plant, not about you). Analytics are keyed to the anonymous
    identifier. We use this under our legitimate interest in improving the product.
  </p>

  <h3>Crash and error reports</h3>
  <p>
    The app uses Firebase Crashlytics (Google) to collect crash reports: technical details such as
    device model, OS version, and stack traces. Server errors may include the anonymous
    identifier.
  </p>

  <h3>Anti-abuse verification</h3>
  <p>
    To keep bots and abusers away from the AI features, we process technical signals that verify
    requests come from a genuine, unmodified copy of the app on a real device. These checks rely
    on device attestation services operated by our platform providers.
  </p>

  <h3>Server logs and usage counters</h3>
  <p>
    Our servers keep short-lived technical logs and usage counters (keyed to the anonymous
    identifier) for rate limiting and abuse prevention. These expire automatically within a few
    days at most. We do not log your photos.
  </p>

  <h2>Legal bases</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR we process data based on: <strong>performance of a contract</strong> (providing
    identification, diagnosis, and your subscription) and <strong>legitimate interest</strong>
    (product analytics, crash reporting, abuse prevention).
  </p>

  <h2>Who processes data for us</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Apple (USA): App Store distribution, payments, device services, iCloud</li>
    <li>Google Firebase (USA): anonymous identity, app integrity, crash reporting</li>
    <li>RevenueCat (USA): subscription status</li>
    <li>PostHog (EU-hosted): product analytics</li>
    <li>AI service providers (USA): analysis of plant photos</li>
    <li>Cloud hosting and database providers (USA and EU): server infrastructure</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where providers are located in the USA, transfers rely on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework
    and/or Standard Contractual Clauses. We will name the specific providers behind these
    categories on request.
  </p>

  <h2>How long we keep data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Plant photos: not retained on our servers (processed and discarded)</li>
    <li>Rate-limit and usage counters: a few days at most</li>
    <li>Cached plant species information: temporarily (contains no personal data)</li>
    <li>
      Analytics and crash data: per the retention settings of the respective tool, kept only as
      long as needed for the purposes above
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You have the right to access, rectify, delete, restrict, or receive a copy of your data, and
    to object to processing based on legitimate interest. Because we hold no account for you,
    exercising these rights works by email: write to
    <a href="mailto:contact@rubenspessoa.dev">contact@rubenspessoa.dev</a>. You also have the
    right to complain to the Austrian Data Protection Authority (
    <a href="https://www.dsb.gv.at" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">dsb.gv.at</a>) or
    your local supervisory authority.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>Arbora is not directed at children under 14, and we do not knowingly collect data from them.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If we change this policy, we will update it here with a new effective date. Material changes
    will be highlighted in the app.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
